{"format_version":"vbulletin-3+-thread","url":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/1369429-the-promised-peril-moonsilver-reaper-daiklave-4-dots","id":1369429,"title":"The Promised Peril, Moonsilver Reaper Daiklave, 4-dots","path":["Home","Onyx Path","White Wolf Worlds","Exalted"],"posts":[{"date":"2020-02-21T13:05:03-08:00","id":1369429,"title":"The Promised Peril, Moonsilver Reaper Daiklave, 4-dots","content":"<b>The Promised Peril</b><b> (Moonsilver Reaper Daiklave, 4-dots)</b><br /> <br /> They say that one scratch from the Promised Peril can kill a man. Maybe it's poisoned, or diseased. Maybe it banishes, destroys, or even consumes the very soul. Some say you'll die screaming; others say your corpse will fall to the earth without a sound. Those who have faced it in battle and lived to tell the tale, shiver as they recall how merely looking at the naked bladed filled them with the fear of death.<br /> <br /> This sword scares even the swordsman's own allies. The swordsman can be the most trusted and beloved person around, but merely carrying this sword around in its sheath lends weight to her threats.<br /> <br /> Unsheathing the sword the first time in a scene is a Threat. If unsheathed when joining battle, or with a Ready Weapon action after battle is already joined, then it doubles as a reflexive Threat action. If unsheathed outside of battle merely as a stunt to a Threat action... [???]<br /> <br /> While the sword is unsheathed, other people are more susceptible to social influence from the swordsman, as long as it involves Instilling or Inspiring fear, making Threats, or Persuading others to cease hostilities or refrain from starting any. However, as the menace of the sword makes it hard to believe her sincerity in any other matter, characters find it easier to resist any other forms of social influence from the swordsman. The potency of this double-edged effect is proportionate to fear for the sword and its reputation.<br /> <br /> Anyone with a Tie or Phobia of fear for the sword loses it the moment that they attune to it, as the sword reveals its true nature to them: It has no power but fear itself. The more they feared the sword, the more this revelation helps them to understand and master it in that very moment.<br /> <br /> The first wielder needed to work hard to build the Promised Peril's reputation, but a new wielder can enjoy benefits that they might have otherwise needed the sword's later Evocations to enjoy. If a new wielder only ever meets people who have never heard of the Promised Peril, then the sword's lethality seems to grow tremendously with the swordsman's mastery. Those mentally scared with terror of the Promised Peril's absolute curse of death, if confronting a new wielder, reason that inexperience and slight mistakes are the only reason why the sword hasn't yet killed them where they stand.<br /> <br /> <br /> <b>Grab The Tiger's Tail:</b> Because the sword inspires fear for itself rather than for you, people may fear the sword far more than you. Therefore, many will seek to disarm you, or contrive situations to force you to leave the sword behind, to neutralize the true threat. The sword offers you no special defense against being disarmed and no way to conveniently follow you everywhere, but it does have an Evocation that you can activate reflexively and maintain indefinitely. The sword radiates an aura of danger that frightens people into keeping their hands to themselves, even if they resist the influence. However, someone who overcomes the influence and attunes to the sword will benefit from the greater enlightenment to its true power; they will instantly learn this Evocation for free.<br /> <br /> <b>Even Toothless Lions Roar:</b> Beating all opponents at Join Battle or successfully stripping Initiative away from them with withering attacks should fill targets with fear for the sword, especially if that's the moment you first draw the sword in the scene. If this Evocation supplements a withering attack, it is more effective when you reduce the target to less than your own Initiative. Actually crashing an opponent would be more effective still.<br /> <br /> <b>Scatter the Herd:</b> After killing one enemy, or influencing one to flee, surrender, or hide, the swordsman either reflexively or (with their next turn) more easily uses social influence to convince any of their remaining allies to do the same. This Evocation can also be triggered by successfully using Spurious Reputation. Even if the targets resist the influence, they all find their Defenses compromised like Black Claw Form -- anyone with a Tie of Fear for the Promised Peril penalizes the Defense of an ally in close range of themselves, as they begin to fear their terrified allies will turn against them to appease the wielder of the Promised Peril.<br /> <br /> <b>Spurious Reputation:</b> Rather than offering or enhancing any decisive attacks, this sword might provide an expensive gambit that carves an illusion into the minds of the target and all witnesses. A defeated target has no further chance to resist, and all witnesses have their ability to resist hindered by any fear they feel for the sword. Perhaps Evocation this can also enhance Grab The Tiger's Tail, so that anyone who fails to overcome that Evocation's influence is likewise convinced that they saw someone else try to take up the sword and die the instant they touched it.<br /> <br /> <br /> <b>Attunement Bonus:</b><br /> If someone with a Tie of fear for the Promised Peril takes up the sword, she instantly loses that Tie. ?(Anyone who witnesses with, if they also have a Tie of fear for the Promised Peril, gains a minor Tie of awe or envy for the sword's new owner, as appropriate.)?<br /> <br /> Visibly bearing the sword gives you a bonus to Threaten actions if people recognize the sword and have a Tie of fear towards it.<br /> <br /> Unsheathing the weapon for the first time in the scene counts as a reflexive Threaten action for the purpose of convincing an opponent to surrender, flee, or at least not initiate or continue hostilities; this action does not suffer penalties for targeting multiple opponents, and doesn't require the swordsman to speak a word.<br /> <br /> <br /> <b>Grab The Tiger's Tail</b><br /> <i>5m; Reflexive; Indefinite; Essence 1; No Prerequisites</i><br /> <br /> [modeled with Threaten] Anyone not attuned to the sword suffers a reflexive Threaten action if they attempt to pick it up, with their Resolve penalized by the intensity of any Tie of fear they might have for the sword. Anyone who fails to resist cannot bring themselves to touch it for an entire day, and is subjected to the same social influence the next time they try.<br /> <br /> [modeled with Persuasion] Anyone not attuned to the sword suffers a reflexive Persuade action if they attempt to pick it up, targeting any Intimacy of fear for the sword. Anyone without such a Tie is treated as though they had one at minor intensity; this virtual Intimacy only serves as an avenue for the Promised Peril to influence that person, and does not count towards any of this Evocation's other effects. Anyone who fails to resist cannot bring themselves to touch it for an entire day, and is subjected to the same social influence the next time they try.<br /> <br /> [either way] Anyone who spends Willpower to resist this influence is still affected, but to a lesser degree. Outside of combat, they may not take up the sword until after an hour passes, plus one more hour for each level of intensity in any Tie of Fear they have for the sword. If engaged in combat, they may not take up the sword until their next turn, plus one more turn for each level of intensity in any Tie of Fear they have for the sword.<br /> <br /> Someone who loses a Tie of fear for the Promised Peril by attuning to it not only loses that Tie, but also gains a temporary Willpower point, even if it would take them over their maximum. If that Tie was Major, the new master of the Promised Peril may additionally go into experience debt to instantly learn this Evocation. Overcoming a Defining Tie allows her to do the same for an additional Evocation at the same time.<br /> <br /> The swordsman may alternatively learn this Evocation for free after a battle in which she involuntarily dropped or was disarmed of the Promised Peril but none of her enemies managed to take it up for themselves before she recovered it.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>formerly Tornado Wolf, formerly Inugami","edited":{"userid":636,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/636-sunder-the-gold","user":"Sunder the Gold","date":". 02-21-2020, 04:07 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/636-sunder-the-gold","name":"Sunder the Gold","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar636_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"5515"}]},"thankedby":[]}]}